{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgCpT4iL1847RE6PQtFPK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The O3 framework wraps a dynamic `import()` in `getAsyncLifecycle`, which returns a thunk. The import only runs when the framework calls that thunk, which happens when a page or extension is first needed.\n\n**How it works**\n\n1. **The app defines the lazy lifecycle.** In its `index.ts`, an app exports `root = getAsyncLifecycle(() => import('./root.component'), options)`. The documented example is in `packages/framework/esm-framework/docs/functions/getAsyncLifecycle.md`.\n2. **`getAsyncLifecycle` returns a thunk, not a component.** It returns `() => lazy().then(({ default }) => getLifecycle(Component, options))`, so nothing is imported until the thunk is called.\n3. **`getLifecycle` builds the single-spa lifecycle.** It wraps the component with `openmrsComponentDecorator` and passes it to `singleSpaReact`.\n4. **The routes loader resolves `routes.json` references.** `loadLifeCycles(appName, componentName)` uses `importDynamic` to load the app's bundle, calls `startupApp()` once, then calls the named export. It returns an empty no-op lifecycle and logs a warning if the app or export is missing.\n5. **Pages and extensions call the loader lazily.** Pages pass `() => loadLifeCycles(page.appName, page.component)` to `registerApplication`. Extensions pass `load: () => loadLifeCycles(appName, extension.component)` to `registerExtension`. Modals and workspaces use the same loader.\n\n`getSyncLifecycle` is the non-lazy variant for components already in the bundle. `getAsyncExtensionLifecycle` is a deprecated alias of `getAsyncLifecycle`.\n\nI did not check the `importDynamic` implementation in `esm-dynamic-loading`, nor the `openmrsComponentDecorator` internals.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-react-utils/src/getLifecycle.ts\", \"startLine\": 52, \"endLine\": 57},\n  {\"path\